From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 17:10:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: the browser drops DRM master, because nothing else
 can

Every Ctrl+Alt+F<n> on a tty was refused behind `RelinquishDisplayControlDrm
drop master failed`, and patch `0017`'s VT switcher was right to refuse it: it
will not acknowledge a switch it could not release the display for. What was
wrong sat one process out.

THE KERNEL FORBIDS IT BY CONSTRUCTION -- not a machine, a sandbox or a
permission bit. The browser opens the card (`OpenDrmDevice`); on a bare tty
nothing else holds master so that open takes it, and `drm_set_master` records
`was_master = true` (`drm_auth.c`). `drm_file_update_pid` then refuses to
refresh the pid on a file that was ever master (`drm_file.c`), deliberately, so
that `drm_master_check_perm` keeps working -- the pid is frozen as the
browser's, permanently. The fd is then MOVED to the GPU process
(`DrmWrapper::ToScopedFD`), and `SCM_RIGHTS` shares the one `struct drm_file`
rather than making a new one, so the frozen pid travels with it.
`drm_dropmaster_ioctl` checks it and answers -EACCES forever.

`drmSetMaster` IS EQUALLY DOOMED, which is why both loops go rather than one:
it runs the same permission check BEFORE its already-master early-out, so
leaving it in place and relying on it to no-op does not work.

A `dup` shares that same `struct drm_file`, so a drop through one kept in the
browser takes effect for the GPU's copy, and the caller's tgid is the recorded
one. `DrmMaster` is that: a card's sysfs path to the browser's own descriptor,
gaining one wherever `DrmDisplayHostManager` hands a card over and losing it
on removal.

THE ORDERING IS THE PART THAT CARRIES A DECISION, and it is not symmetric.
Relinquish is GPU-first: the GPU detaches planes while this process still holds
master, and `GpuRelinquishedDisplayControl` drops only once told that half
succeeded -- a GPU half that failed leaves us master on purpose, because
releasing a display it could not stand down from is the two-owner bug arriving
by the other door. Take is the mirror: master first, and the GPU is not asked
at all if it failed. A drop that fails fails the whole relinquish even when the
GPU succeeded, because the switcher's `VT_RELDISP(0)` is the thing that keeps
the console off a display Chromium is still scanning out on.

THE SYSCALL MOVES; THE GPU'S BOOKKEEPING STAYS, and the difference between
those two is a live GPU process. `has_master()` is not cosmetic:
`DrmWindow::SchedulePageFlip` reads it to decide whether a frame is worth
committing, and left saying yes while the console belongs to somebody else it
commits one. The atomic plane manager has no EACCES exemption -- the legacy one
does, atomic is what a modern driver uses -- so the commit fails, arms
`PageFlipWatchdog`, and its fifteen-second timer is `LOG(FATAL) << "Failed to
modeset ... Crashing GPU process."` Only a modeset disarms it and nothing
modesets on the way back from a VT switch, so the process would reliably kill
itself. `DrmWrapper::AssumeMaster` is therefore what the two loops call now:
the flag without the ioctl, because on this platform the transition is decided
in another process.

`TakeDisplayControl` also rebuilds the window mapping unconditionally.
`IsFastDrmMasterDropEnabled()` is off by default and so the relinquish does not
tear it down today, but nothing else would rebuild it if it were on --
`DrmVtSwitcher` calls `NativeDisplayDelegate::TakeDisplayControl` directly
rather than through `DisplayConfigurator`, so no reconfiguration follows a
switch back. It is a no-op when there is nothing to rebuild.

Upstream's `RelinquishDisplayControlFail` is gone rather than adjusted: it
disabled fast drop and made the card refuse a drop, and with the ioctl out of
this process there is nothing left in that path to fail. The failing drop is
the browser's now, and `DrmMasterTest` covers it. The other three cases keep
the flag transitions they always asserted.

TESTED WHERE THE BOOKKEEPING IS. `DrmMaster` takes the syscall injected, for
the reason `StepVtSwitch` takes the tty: neither `drmSetMaster` nor
`drmDropMaster` can be exercised without a real card this process alone opened,
while losing track of one descriptor is what turns a switch into a wedge. Five
cases, written first and watched failing: every card is asked, every card is
still asked after one refuses (stopping would strand a second card in the
opposite state from the first), a card that went away is not touched, and a
drop with nothing held is a refusal rather than a vacuous yes -- the only way
to arrive there empty is that the card was never recorded, and answering yes
to that releases a display still being scanned out on.

`DrmMasterTest:5` is a floor in both workflows and the filtered run names the
suite, so it actually executes.
